in this episode of cloud performance  atlas I helped some toy makers with  their boot times will performance be the  hot toy this holiday season stay tuned  to find out capital fun is an IOT toy  developer who has been converting over  portions of their architecture to  serverless systems however as their  google cloud functions uses didn't scale  to meet demand as fast as they wanted  they quickly pinned cold boot time as  the culprit and reached out to us to see  how they could improve it which totally  makes sense I even though GCF is  considered a serverless architecture you  still have to be aware that there are  some cold boot time overhead as new  instances are spun up now most of the  time your function will already be  running an instance and will be ready to  handle future triggers as fast as  possible however your functions might be  in a cold state because number one your  function has been deployed but not  triggered yet or number two your  function has been idle long enough that  it was recycled for resources or number  three your function is auto-scaling to  handle capacity and creating a new  instance in any of these cases your code  isn't currently running and the next  trigger event will spin up a new  instance now when it comes to GCF the  number one contributor to slow cold boot  performance is dependencies when a  module boots no Jess resolves all the  require calls from the entry point using  synchronous i/o which can take a  significant amount of time if the number  of dependencies are large or if the  content itself requires a lot of linking  so when I saw that there's GCF module  had 50 dependencies well we knew where  to focus our efforts as a first step we  worked with their tech team to trim out  any dependencies that might not be  needed this includes areas where they  might be using one function from a  library but including the entire library  so after a lot of negotiation digging  and spaghetti code clinging we were able  to get down to eight packages resulting  in a 2x performance in cold boot time  which was good but there was still work  to do next up we looked at what  dependency versions their functions were  actually using more popular versions of  a package are most likely already stored  in gcf's dependency cache  meaning that importing and resolving can  be done much quicker so once we unified  of capital funds dependencies to use the  same versions that were more likely to  be used in the cache we saw another nice  performance boost finally digging  through the node.js code we decided to  set up lazy loading of core requirements  rather than having them all occur in  cold boot phase this allowed us to  require only what's needed at the start  and then switch to an async version of  the require keyword later for specific  request handling bringing down the cold  boot times even further with these small  changes we saw a 10x improvement in  capital funds called boot time which  isn't bad for a day's work and you want  to know more about the changes check out  the article in the description below and  don't forget to check out the rest of  our cloud performance Atlas content  because when it comes to performance  every millisecond counts
 

